{
  "model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "manuscript_hash": "1e4330669b265585dc315938382079bfd801a98e603b586ab4b9d829a5316242",
  "run": "writeup-referee-zt-neutral-r2-0-20261104-080427-319919",
  "review": {
    "verdict": "revise",
    "summary": "The mathematics is correct and faithful to the archive. I checked every proof step of Propositions 3.1\u20138.1 and Theorem 1.1 against the archived P1\u2013P9 statements and proofs. All hypotheses, ranges, quantifiers, error terms, exponents and \u03b7 = min(\u03b4/12, 1/(6d)) are unchanged. The listed local corrections are valid and none of them weakens anything. I found no gap in the source argument, so there is no source-gap.\n\nThe numerics also hold up:\n- I re-ran the shipped reproduce.py on a scratch copy of the workspace, reusing the re-run snapshot results. It regenerated every file in generated/ byte-identically.\n- The PDF and all artifact hashes match build-inputs.json.\n- The shipped snapshot re-run equals the archived results.json except for timing fields.\n- My own independent brute-force code reproduces 8 archived V_f rows exactly, the C_f partial products, Vaughan's c_2 and the exact-shape values N_MT.\n\nEvery citation checks against the supplied PDFs, and the PDF is clean and readable.\n\nThe revise verdict rests only on minor provenance and traceability items:\n- Appendix B leaves out the write-up review stage: the reviewer models recorded in provenance.json, and the fact that some corrections came from the write-up referees.\n- Appendix B does not separate corrections covered by the recorded operator-audit / Markus entry from those added later, notably the added hypothesis in Lemma 2.6.\n- changes.md cites round-1 feedback files that were not supplied.",
    "issues": [
      "Provenance, Appendix B (sections/B-provenance.tex, 'Models and roles' and 'Workflow'): the write-up workflow is incomplete. Only the writer (claude-opus-5-5, Claude Code 2.1.311) is named. provenance.json also records reviewer_models [claude-opus-5-5, codex:gpt-6.1-sol] and a writer-side codex-cli 0.166.0, and the write-up went through referee rounds that changed the text. Add one sentence naming the write-up reviewer models and saying there was a round of revision.",
      "Distinguishing archived work from later corrections, Appendix B 'Workflow' (\"local corrections, which are carried out in this write-up ... together with the cosmetic points raised by the referees\"): this reads as if every correction came from the operator audit or the run's referees. It hides that Lemma 2.6's extra hypothesis (changes.md \u00a74 item 19) and the 449/8 arithmetic fix (item 20) came from the write-up review. The archived P5 Lemma EM, case b = \u221e, is false as stated (the cos(log u)/u counterexample is valid). Every archived application satisfies the added hypothesis, so the P5 argument and all statements stand; this is not a source gap. But the correction is not among those recorded under Markus's 2026-11-03 entry in interventions.md (P1(iii), P8 endpoint, unused P6/P8 notes). Appendix B should say in one line that Lemma 2.6 narrows an over-general auxiliary lemma of the archived P5 proof, introduced at the write-up stage, and changes.md \u00a74 item 19 should flag it for Markus's acknowledgement.",
      "changes.md (\u00a7\u00a70, 4 items 18\u201320, \u00a74a, \u00a77) cites `feedback/0.json` and `feedback/1.json` as the source of several corrections, but these files are not in the supplied workspace, so those attributions cannot be checked. Either note in changes.md that they lie outside the supplied archive, or quote the relevant objection inline.",
      "Cosmetic: the runtime statements disagree. README.md says 'roughly 12\u201315 minutes', \u00a710.1 says 'roughly 12 minutes and 5 GB', and reproduction.json records 651 s for the snapshot and 700 s in total. Make them consistent, or point only to reproduction.json as the README already does elsewhere.",
      "Optional wording, Appendix B: 'The project owner accepted the proof as correct and complete.' The recorded words in interventions.md are 'Let's just assume the proof is correct and complete', and the operator audit 'leaves final mathematical acceptance to Markus'. Quoting the recorded wording would be more precise."
    ],
    "statement_checks": "I compared Theorem 1.1(i)\u2013(iii) with plan.md 'Main theorem' and the P9 statement:\n- Same class (f \u2208 Z[t], d \u2265 1, f \u2265 2 and f' \u2265 1 for real t \u2265 1), same weight f'(k), same range x \u2265 2, y \u2265 1, Y = f(y) \u2264 x.\n- Same error terms x^{1/3+\u03b5}Y^{5/3} + x^{5/3+\u03b5} + x^{1/2+\u03b5}Y^{3/2\u22121/(2d)} and x^{5/3+\u03b5} + x^{1/2}Y.\n- Same range x^{7/9+\u03b4} \u2264 f(y) \u2264 x^{1\u2212\u03b4} for \u03b4 \u2208 (0, 1/9), and same \u03b7 = min(\u03b4/12, 1/(6d)).\n- C_f and \u03c3_p (the archived \u03c4_p) are identical. I checked a_p, b_p, c_p, the difference identities of Lemma 8.2 and Remark 1.3's \u03c3_p|_{\u03c1\u22611} = C_{1,p}(1\u2212p^{-2})\u00b2 symbolically: the difference is 0.\n- 'Real t' is made explicit. That is the reading the archived P8/P9 proofs need (monotonicity on [1, \u221e)) and the one Br\u00fcdern\u2013Wooley use. Under an integer-t reading the class would be larger; I note this as an interpretation, not an error.\n\nPropositions 3.1, 3.3, 4.1, 5.1, 6.1, 6.2, 7.1 and 8.1 match P1\u2013P8. I verified every strengthening listed in changes.md \u00a74a against the archived proof lines:\n- P6 for all x > 0 and q \u2265 1 (P6 proof lines 50 and 71).\n- c_1 = 60 (P5 line 37).\n- c_0 = 3e^{2\u03b6(3/2)} (P4 line 68).\n- Lemma 3.2 (P4 lines 45\u201348).\n- |\u03b8| < 1 in Lemma 2.2, with a correct proof.\n- Constant 3 + 9C(\u03b5) in Proposition 5.1, from 2(1+\u03c4*) + 1 + 7\u03c4*.\n\nI checked the corrections themselves:\n- P1(iii): normalisation and convergence (|u(p)| \u2264 1 for p | q) are correct.\n- P3 Step 3: the left side equals (2\u03c0\u00b2/(3z))\u03a31/e, and 2\u03c0\u00b2/3 \u2248 6.58 \u2264 7.\n- P5(b): R_1, \u2026, R_5 bounds re-derived; total (1/8 + 272/12 + 12 + 32/3 + 32/3)\u039b = 449\u039b/8 < 57\u039b, plus \u039b/4, < 60\u039b.\n- P8 endpoint: Lemma 8.5 on arbitrary bounded intervals is correct. The error is in fact at most 2 sup, so 3 sup is safe.\n- Lemma 2.6 at b = \u221e: the counterexample is valid, and the added hypothesis holds for u^{-s} (Re s > 1) and for \u03a6.\n\nRe-derived by hand:\n- The exact identity (Proposition 4.1), including the case N = 0 and the cancellation of x\u00b2S_2(q) and 6x/\u03c0\u00b2.\n- P6's factorisation m_t = t_0\u00b2t_1 and the local factors a_p, b_p, c_p.\n- P8's w = \u03bc*h\u266e bounds, \u03c1(e) \u2264 C_P d^{\u03c9(e)} b, the tail c_4W^{-1/2}, the short sum W^{1/4+\u03b5}(1 + log W) and the Euler product \u03c3_p/a_p.\n- The \u03b4/\u03b7 bookkeeping in \u00a79.3.\n\nLimitation: I did not re-derive \u03b6(s)'s continuation beyond the Euler\u2013Maclaurin step shown.",
    "dependency_checks": "Every application stays within its contract:\n- Prop 7.1(i) uses Prop 4.1 (x \u2265 1, all q), Prop 5.1 with m = hq \u2208 [1, x] and x \u2265 2, Prop 3.3(i)/(ii) (q \u2264 x is not needed), and x^{3/2} \u2264 x^{5/3+\u03b5}.\n- Prop 7.1(ii) adds Prop 6.2. The constant c_7 = max(2c_3 + 2c_0, c_6) is correct.\n- \u00a79 uses 2 \u2264 f(k) \u2264 Y \u2264 x, from f' \u2265 1 on real [1, \u221e), so Prop 7.1 applies to every q = f(k) uniformly.\n- Prop 8.1(b),(c) are used with 1 + log Y \u2264 C'(\u03b5)x^\u03b5. Prop 8.1(a) at \u03b5/3 is combined with y^{-1/2} \u2264 \u03b3_0^{1/(2d)}Y^{-1/(2d)}. T_3 \u2264 x^{1/2}Y^{3/2\u22121/(2d)} because Y \u2265 2 and d \u2265 1. The combined constant c(f,\u03b5) is correctly composed.\n- In \u00a79.3, \u03b5 = min(\u03b4/12, 1/(9d)) gives the three ratios \u2264 x^{\u2212\u03b4/12}, \u2264 x^{\u221217\u03b4/12} and \u2264 x^{\u22125/(18d)}, all \u2264 x^{\u2212\u03b7}.\n\nProp 6.2 uses Prop 6.1(c) with Z = Z_1 > 0, valid for all Z. Prop 6.1(c) uses (b) for every positive argument, the bound \u03a3_{2/3} \u2264 C_\u03b2 2^{\u03c9(q)}, and rearrangement justified by \u03a3_m B(W/m\u00b2) \u2264 3\u221aW.\n\nMerged arguments:\n- Lemma 2.1 (tail bound, all w > 0) and Lemma 2.2 (residue classes) cover every archived use.\n- Lemma 2.5 (coprime pairs, grouped by n = ab) gives \u220f_{p\u2224e}(1 \u2212 2/p\u00b2) and serves both P1(iv) and P3 Step 4.\n- Lemma 3.2 (T_r for all y > 0) gives P4(i) as the case r = 1 with the same constant 3.\n- Lemma 2.4 collects the facts on \u03b7, \u03ba and A_0. Its bound MT \u2264 6x/\u03c0\u00b2 is correct.\n\nThe integration reports' 'composes = true' agrees with my own check. I found no interface mismatch.",
    "numerics_checks": "Path: reproduce.py checks the SHA-256 of compute.py and counts.cpp against accepted.json, re-runs them unchanged and compares the output with the archive. The shipped generated/snapshot_rerun_results.json differs from source/numerics/snapshot/results.json only in runtime fields.\n\nMy independent checks (own code, in scratch-referee/):\n- Direct brute-force V_f, from residue-class counts and g taken from its local factors, for 8 rows: k\u00b3+2 at x = 2.5e5 with r = 0.01, 0.1, 1; k\u00b2+1 at 2.5e5 and 1e6 with r = 0.01, 0.1; k\u00b3+2 at 1e6 with r = 0.01; 2k+1 at 2.5e5 with r = 0.01. All agree with the archived V to relative difference \u2264 2.2e-16.\n- An independent MT implementation (direct t-sum plus Euler-product tails) reproduces Table 5's N_MT (0.14171, 0.15191, 0.15361, 0.15499) and V/\u03a3_MT (1.00104, 0.999999, 0.99972, 1.00024).\n- Independent C_f partial products at p \u2264 1e5, with \u03c1(p) by brute force mod p and \u03c1(p\u00b2) by brute force at p = 2, 3: 0.138083068, 0.135607954, 0.142613765, 0.128479079, and Vaughan's c_2 at 0.128478873. All match Table 1.\n- I = \u22124\u03b6(\u22121/2) = \u03b6(3/2)/\u03c0 = 0.8315448999.\n\nRe-running the shipped reproduce.py on a scratch copy (--reuse-work) regenerated all 27 generated files byte-identically, including macros.tex and every table. reproduction.json differs only in the timing fields. The PDF and artifact SHA-256s match build-inputs.json.\n\nThe tail bound is rigorous: 8K(N^{-2} + (30N)^{-1})/(1 \u2212 K/N\u00b2) with K = 3 + 2d and P_0 = \u2205. Endpoints are rounded outwards, and the fixed digits (0.1380826, 0.135607, 0.142613) are correct for the printed intervals. Floating-point rounding is disclosed as uncertified.\n\nProse numbers all match the generated data:\n- stability 0.91% and 0.04%;\n- N between 0.140 and 0.145 at r = 0.005, and between 0.167 and 0.184 at r = 1;\n- \u03b2 between 1.538 and 1.551;\n- relative differences \u22122.61%, +0.53%, \u22120.65%, all inside the envelopes;\n- sensitivity range 0.134\u20130.142 brackets C_f;\n- x^{-2/9} \u2248 0.0135, \u03b4_row \u2264 2.3e-4 at r = 1, and maximum 0.103 at r = 0.1;\n- x^{-1/3}r^{-3/2} \u2248 1.6 and 1.6e-3;\n- V_f/\u03a3_MT within [0.9938, 1.0010] over 66 rows.\n\nThe fits and envelopes are the snapshot's own and are correctly labelled heuristic. Every displayed number is covered by generated files.\n\nLimitations: I did not repeat the full 11-minute NTT re-run myself. My check of it rests on the identical results.json and the 8 brute-force rows. A first, slow version of my C_f check (sympy gcd root counting) hit its time limit and was stopped. The faster replacement listed above completed and agrees.",
    "citation_checks": "Checked against the supplied PDFs:\n- Vaughan 2005, rendered pp. 574\u2013575: Theorem (1.12)\u2013(1.13); c_k from (1.14) with k = 2 gives 8C_1(\u2212\u03b6(\u22121/2))/(3\u03b6(2)\u00b2); C_1 from (1.15) with k = 2 gives (p\u22121)\u00b2p^{3/2} \u2212 2p\u2074 + 3p\u00b3 \u2212 p over p\u00b2(p\u00b2\u22121)\u00b2; Q_1 = x^{2/3}exp(\u2212(2/3)c\u0303\u2026) from (1.16); the earlier x\u00b2\ud835\udcd5 results in (1.6)\u2013(1.11); 'x sufficiently large'; and reference [1] = BGPVW.\n- BGPVW 1998, \u00a71, PDF p. 3: Evelyn\u2013Linfoot's elementary binary result r_k(n) = S_k(n)n + O(n^{2/(k+1)+\u03b5}) is quoted accurately.\n- Br\u00fcdern\u2013Wooley (preprint PDF, 2009): the Theorem for degree \u2265 2 with weight f'(k); the approach is attributed to Goldston\u2013Vaughan [3] and Vaughan [10], whose bibliography entries match the manuscript's.\n- Montgomery\u2013Vaughan 2007: Theorem 1.8 (product of Dirichlet series), Theorem 1.9 (Euler product), \u00a71.3 / Theorem 1.12 (continuation to \u03c3 > 0), (2.20) (d(n) \u2264 C(\u03b5)n^\u03b5) and Corollary 10.4 (functional equation). \u03b6(\u22121/2) = \u2212\u03b6(3/2)/(4\u03c0) follows correctly.\n\nAll cited works are in papers/. Br\u00fcdern\u2013Wooley 2011 is the only post-2010 item, as permitted. DOIs come from papers/INDEX.md. For BGPVW and Br\u00fcdern\u2013Wooley, no DOI exists in INDEX.md or bibliography-candidates.json, and the bib notes say so rather than inventing one. Mirsky was correctly dropped as unverifiable.\n\nLimitations: Goldston\u2013Vaughan 1997 and Vaughan 1998 are cited only through Br\u00fcdern\u2013Wooley's attribution; I did not open their PDFs. The supplied BGPVW PDF is a 2026 retypeset copy, so the page '3' is that copy's page, not the journal page. I did no network search and read no excluded or post-2010 source.",
    "provenance_checks": "Appendix B matches provenance.json:\n- repository URL (stated only, not browsed), archive label zt-neutral, archive commit ed0aebaf\u2026, harness commit 33aadc60\u2026, run revision 8dfc53d0\u2026;\n- roles: planner and probe Opus/Sol, probe also Sonnet; numerics Sonnet/Sol with Sol accepted; prover Sonnet; referees Opus and Sol; integrators Opus and Sol;\n- CLI versions Claude Code 2.1.309 and codex-cli 0.166.0, and writer claude-opus-5-5 on Claude Code 2.1.311;\n- the version caveat.\n\nIt claims no human authorship; the author line says 'archived machine-generated proof run'. The workflow facts agree with interventions.md: eight plans, simplest@opus selected by the owner, all nine propositions passing both referees in round 1, both integrators reporting that the propositions compose, the operator audit, and the numerics accepted on computational grounds. Independent checks attributed to the run (Sol: direct counts of the 8 rows for each of k\u00b2+1 and k\u00b3+2 at x = 250000; Opus: an independent \u03a3 f'MT) appear in the assembly reports. changes.md maps every result to its archived file and line and lists both reviews per proposition; I checked these against source/props/*/review-v1-*.json (all pass, severities none or cosmetic, as stated).\n\nGaps, listed under issues:\n- The write-up reviewer models in provenance.json and the write-up review round are not recorded in Appendix B.\n- Corrections that originated in write-up review (Lemma 2.6, 449/8) are not distinguished there from the run/operator-audit corrections recorded under Markus's entry.\n- The feedback/*.json files are not supplied.\n\nCompliance: I read nothing outside the workspace, nothing by the excluded author or the excluded paper, and no other run's write-ups. My scratch files are in scratch-referee/, which includes a scratch copy of manuscript/ and of the needed numerics files for the re-run. No supplied file was modified.",
